[Event "Petroff: 3.Nxe5"]

{The Petroff (2...Nf6) counterattacks e4 instead of defending e5.}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... e5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. Nf3 {Attacks e5. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2... Nf6 {[%cal Gf6e4] The Petroff. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
3. Nxe5 {Takes the pawn on e5, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
3... d6 {First kick the knight. 3...Nxe4? 4.Qe2 is a known trap.}
4. Nf3 {Moves the knight out of attack to f3 and controls e5 and d4.}
 (4. Nc4 {A rarer retreat. The knight goes to c4 and controls e5. Black's last move put a pawn on d6, which this now pressures.}
4... Nxe4 {Takes the pawn on e4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
5. Qe2 {Pins along the e-file. The queen goes to e2 and eyes the knight on e4. That knight is currently undefended.}
5... Qe7 {Breaks the pin and offers a queen trade. The queen goes to e7 and controls e5 and supports the knight on e4.}
)
4... Nxe4 {Now takes e4. Takes the pawn on e4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
5. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
 (5. Nc3 {Trades knights. Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and eyes the knight on e4. That knight is currently undefended.}
5... Nxc3 {Takes the knight on c3; White will want to recapture.}
6. dxc3 {Recaptures, opening lines. Recaptures on c3, restoring the material balance.}
6... Be7 {Develops the bishop to e7, where it controls f6 and supports the pawn on d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
7. Be3 {Develops the bishop to e3, where it controls d4.}
7... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls d4 and e5.}
8. Qd2 {Prepares O-O-O. The queen goes to d2 and controls d4 and d5.}
8... Be6 {Develops the bishop to e6, where it controls d5.}
)
5... d5 {Supports the knight. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c4 and supports the knight on e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
6. Bd3 {Develops the bishop to d3, where it eyes the knight on e4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls e5 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
7. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
7... Be7 {Develops the bishop to e7, where it controls f6 and d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
8. c4 {Hits d5. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and eyes the pawn on d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
8... Nb4 {Hits the bishop. The knight goes to b4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
9. Be2 {Regroups the bishop to e2.}
9... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.} *

[Event "Petroff: 3.d4"]

{White can also open with 3.d4.}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... e5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. Nf3 {Attacks e5. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2... Nf6 {Petroff. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
3. d4 {Opens. Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and eyes the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
3... Nxe4 {Counterattacks. Takes the pawn on e4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
 (3... exd4 {Opens the centre. Takes the pawn on d4; White will want to recapture.}
4. e5 {Kicks the knight. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d6 and eyes the knight on f6.}
4... Ne4 {Central post. Moves the knight out of attack to e4.}
5. Qxd4 {Regains the pawn. Takes the pawn on d4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
5... d5 {Supports the knight. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c4 and supports the knight on e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
)
4. Bd3 {Develops the bishop to d3, where it eyes the knight on e4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
4... d5 {Supports. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c4 and supports the knight on e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
5. Nxe5 {Takes the pawn on e5, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
5... Nd7 {Challenges. Develops the knight to d7, where it eyes the knight on e5. White's last move put a knight on e5, which this now pressures.}
6. Nxd7 {Takes the knight on d7; Black will want to recapture.}
6... Bxd7 {Recaptures on d7, restoring the material balance.}
7. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
7... Bd6 {Develops the bishop to d6, where it controls e5. That clears the way to castle kingside.} *
